### Summary
The `prefix` field of a Backup (used to namespace backup files within an S3-compatible destination) is validated only as a non-empty string, with no restriction on shell metacharacters, and is interpolated directly into the rclone command line used to upload backups and to prune old ones. The value sits inside a double-quoted rclone argument (or, in the retention-cleanup path, with no quoting at all), so an authenticated organization member with permission to configure backups for a service can inject arbitrary shell commands that execute as root on the Dokploy host (or configured remote server) as soon as a backup runs - manually or on its schedule.

### Details

Field definition: `packages/server/src/db/schema/backups.ts` - `prefix: text("prefix").notNull()`, validated as `z.string().min(1)` with no character restriction (unlike branch-name fields elsewhere in the schema, which do carry a restrictive regex).

`normalizeS3Path()` (`packages/server/src/utils/backups/utils.ts`) only trims whitespace and strips leading/trailing slashes - it does not escape or reject shell metacharacters:
```ts
export const normalizeS3Path = (prefix: string) => {
	const normalizedPrefix = prefix.trim().replace(/^\/+|\/+$/g, "");
	return normalizedPrefix ? `${normalizedPrefix}/` : "";
};
```
Vulnerable sink (identical pattern for every backup type - PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, MongoDB, libSQL, Compose, and the web-server backup), shown for `packages/server/src/utils/backups/postgres.ts:34-40`:
```ts
const { prefix } = backup;
const bucketDestination = `${appName}/${normalizeS3Path(prefix)}${backupFileName}`;
...
const rcloneDestination = `:s3:${destination.bucket}/${bucketDestination}`;
const rcloneCommand = `rclone rcat ${rcloneFlags.join(" ")} "${rcloneDestination}"`;
```
The whole destination path - including the unescaped `prefix` - sits inside a single pair of double quotes as one rclone argument. Bash still performs `$(...)`/backtick command substitution inside double-quoted strings, so it expands and executes any such sequence in `prefix` while constructing that argument, before `rclone` is ever invoked.

An even more direct sink exists in the retention-cleanup path that runs automatically after every successful backup, `packages/server/src/utils/backups/index.ts:139-149` (`keepLatestNBackups`):
```ts
const backupFilesPath = `:s3:${destination.bucket}/${appName}/${normalizeS3Path(backup.prefix)}`;
const rcloneList = `rclone lsf ${rcloneFlags.join(" ")} --include "..." ${backupFilesPath}`;
const rcloneDelete = `rclone delete ${rcloneFlags.join(" ")} ${backupFilesPath}{}`;
const rcloneCommand = `${rcloneList} | ${sortAndPickUnwantedBackups} ${rcloneDelete}`;
```
Here `backupFilesPath` is interpolated with no quoting at all.

Both are ultimately executed via `execAsync`/`execAsyncRemote` (`child_process.exec` under `/bin/sh -c`, or the equivalent over SSH for a remote server).

The authorization gate on creating/updating/running a backup is `checkServicePermissionAndAccess(ctx, serviceId, { backup: ["create"] })` - the default `member` organization role already includes `backup: ["read","create","update","delete","restore"]` for any service that member has access to, so no admin/owner role is required.

### PoC

Tested against the running Dokploy application through the real web UI:

1. Added an S3-compatible Destination (any credentials work for this PoC - the injected command executes while the rclone command line is being constructed, regardless of whether the destination is actually reachable).
2. On an existing PostgreSQL service, created a Backup pointing at that destination, with the "Prefix Destination" field set to:
```
$(touch /tmp/backup_pwn_marker)
```

